      title: "A public register of mining accidents in Türkiye",
      lede: "Fatal mining and quarrying accidents in Türkiye, as far as open sources document them. Field citations and dated evidence notices show the support and unresolved gaps in this published snapshot.",
      note: "<strong>A partial research register.</strong> Missing records do not mean no accidents occurred. <a href=\"#about\">Scope and limits ↓</a>",
      about_h: "Scope and limits",
      about_intro: "The limits of this work, in one place. The notes beside each view above explain how to read that view; everything that holds for the whole register is stated here.",
      ab1_t: "What it is",
      ab1_d: "A partial, source-traceable research register of fatal mining and quarrying accidents, not a complete or validated national record. Published means this register's review rules were met, not that every figure is final. Records still in review are counted in the tiles above but never listed.",
      ab2_t: "Where the values come from",
      ab2_d: "Most records began as entries in Wikidata (CC0) and Wikipedia (text CC BY-SA 4.0). Where an official or professional source states the same toll, the toll is cited to it instead: TTK's own chronology, the Assembly's records and the Chamber of Mining Engineers. Of {n} death tolls, {t1} are cited to an official or parliamentary source, {t2} to the Chamber and {t3} to Wikipedia or the press; that is {d12} of {deaths} recorded deaths on the first two and {d3} on the third. {passages} of {n} toll citations carry the passage that states the figure.",
      ab10_t: "What reviewed means here",
      ab10_d: "Every published value was selected by a recorded decision, and a claim that was not selected stays on file. Most decisions were first made by a rule applied to a batch of claims from the seed sources and authorised once by the project owner; they were not each weighed by a person. The working database was lost on 11 November 2026 and rebuilt from the last public export, so {restored} of the {dec} decisions on file repeat the published selection without their original reasoning. {delegated} decisions since then were prepared by an AI assistant from documents checked one by one, under the owner's delegation, and are marked as such. Any decision can be superseded by a later one, and the earlier one is kept.",
      ab3_t: "What a gap means",
      ab3_d: "A year, province or mine missing here is absent from this register; this does not establish safety or zero deaths. The site layer is a partial inventory from open structured sources. Its completeness and current operating status are not established.",
      ab4_t: "Why the İSİG series is kept separate",
      ab4_d: "İSİG Meclisi reports mining-sector work deaths from all causes; this register counts deaths in selected published incidents. Definitions and coverage differ. The series are separate context: neither subtraction nor their ratio establishes missing incidents or a coverage percentage. Record-level matching and harmonized definitions are required.",
      ab5_t: "What it is not",
      ab5_d: "Not a ranking, not a comparison between provinces or countries, not exposure-adjusted risk, and never a statement of cause or legal responsibility. Reported mechanisms describe what sources say happened. No individual is named anywhere, and no personal data of the people who died is stored or shown. Policy markers describe chronology, not evidence of an effect.",
      ab6_t: "How locations are shown",
      ab6_d: "Few records carry coordinates precise enough for a facility-level mark, and even those are approximate to the facility or its vicinity. Every other record is drawn at a representative point inside its province, which is never the accident site. Boundaries: Natural Earth (public domain), Albers equal-area projection.",
      ab8_t: "Who was in charge",
      ab8_d: "Where a company is named, it is the company the cited source places at the mine on the day of the accident, and the line says which it was: the company that ran the pit, the one that owned it, the licence holder, or a contractor working under the operator. They are not always the same company, which is why each is recorded separately rather than merged into one name. Where one organization appears under two of them, the two words come from two sources describing the same pit, not from two different arrangements. A company is shown as alleged where the source only relays somebody\u2019s claim. Naming a company records who was operating, never who was at fault: courts decide that and this register does not. {n} of {total} records name one; the rest are blank because no assessed source names a company, usually saying only \u201ca private operation\u201d.",
      ab7_t: "Corrections and open work",
      ab7_d: "Analysis methods are documented and provisional. Open tasks include corroboration against official records, a harmonised rates series from TÜİK and TKİ data, and fuller site registers (Global Energy Monitor, MAPEG licence data). Corrections go through the issue templates in the project repository. No personal data of victims is stored or shown.",
      ab9_t: "How it is kept current",
      ab9_d: "Once a month the sources whose assessment permits automated collection are swept again (Wikidata, Wikipedia and the İSİG Meclisi reports) and anything they state that this register does not hold is written into a review queue. The sweep only finds and reports. Nothing it finds becomes a published value until a reviewer decides on the claim, so a candidate waiting in that queue carries no standing here. News sits outside the sweep by design: it was assessed for retrieval article by article rather than crawling, so press reports are followed up by hand during review. Every source last swept: {checked}. Last attempt: {attempted}. Waiting for review: {waiting}. {state} A sweep with no new candidates only reports what that run detected. It does not establish that sources were unchanged or that no accidents occurred.",
